CALIIP — TECHNOLOGY DECISIONS

PURPOSE

This document records the technology choices for Caliip and the reasoning behind them.

The project must remain achievable within 80 development hours.

FRONTEND

Vue 3 + TypeScript
- Required technologies.
- Vue provides reactive components and a straightforward development experience.
- TypeScript improves type safety and maintainability.

Vuetify 3
- Required UI framework.
- Use Vuetify components and utilities for styling.
- Add custom CSS only when necessary.
- Do not introduce Tailwind CSS.

Vue Router
- Provides navigation between daily, weekly, monthly, and settings views.
- Supports browser history and meaningful URLs.

Vue Composables
- Use Vue's ref, computed, and composables for state management.
- Do not add Pinia unless a concrete need emerges.

STORAGE

IndexedDB + Dexie.js
- IndexedDB is the required local persistence mechanism.
- Dexie simplifies database queries, schema management, and transactions.
- Use the existing documented Meal and FoodEntry schema.
- IndexedDB is the source of truth for meal history.
- Do not introduce a remote meal database.

DATA MODEL

Meal:
- id
- date
- name
- source
- createdAt

FoodEntry:
- id
- mealId
- name
- quantityGrams
- caloriesPer100g
- proteinPer100g
- carbsPer100g
- fatPer100g

A meal contains multiple food entries.

Nutritional totals are calculated from food entries rather than stored redundantly.

Use local calendar dates formatted as YYYY-MM-DD.

PWA

vite-plugin-pwa
- Integrates PWA capabilities with Vite.
- Generates the manifest and service worker.
- Supports installation and offline application assets.
- Offline functionality covers existing locally stored data, not external API calls.

DATE HANDLING

date-fns
- Used for date navigation and weekly/monthly calculations.
- Prefer explicit local calendar dates to avoid timezone-related shifts.

TESTING

Vitest
- Integrates naturally with Vite.
- Focus on calorie calculations, date aggregation, and database CRUD.
- Use fake-indexeddb if needed for database tests.
- Avoid extensive testing infrastructure beyond project needs.

BACKEND

Bun
- Runtime for the minimal API server.
- Use Bun's built-in HTTP server and native TypeScript support.
- Avoid unnecessary backend frameworks.

The backend exists only to protect API keys and proxy requests to external services.

Planned endpoints:
- POST /api/analyze-meal — OpenAI meal image analysis.
- GET /api/foods/search — USDA nutritional food search.

The backend must not persist meal history.

Use environment variables for API keys.

Never expose OpenAI or USDA API keys in frontend code.

Validate uploaded images and apply reasonable request limits.

AI

OpenAI API
- Used for image-based food identification and quantity estimation.
- Return structured food names and estimated quantities in grams.
- AI results are estimates, not guaranteed measurements.
- Users must review and edit results before saving.

Use one application-managed OpenAI API key rather than requiring users to supply their own.

NUTRITION DATA

USDA FoodData Central
- Selected as the initial nutritional reference database.
- Offers broad food coverage and a JSON REST API.
- Use the Bun backend to protect the USDA API key.
- Store selected nutritional values on FoodEntry records for offline access.

CIQUAL was considered as an alternative:
- Maintained by ANSES in France.
- Reliable nutritional composition data.
- Particularly relevant to French and European foods.
- Downloadable dataset, but no comparable ready-to-use public search API.
- Would require dataset conversion and normalization.

USDA is the current choice because its search API is convenient and the API key is already available.

VOICE INPUT

Web Speech API
- Preferred over paid transcription APIs.
- Lower implementation complexity.
- Browser support varies.
- Manual input remains available as a fallback.

CAMERA

HTML file input
- Use input type="file" with accept="image/*" and capture="environment".
- Supports camera capture on compatible mobile devices and file selection.
- Avoid implementing a custom live camera preview.

HTTP REQUESTS

Native Fetch API
- Use fetch for frontend-to-backend requests and backend-to-external-service requests.
- Do not introduce Axios.

MONOREPO

Bun Workspaces
- Use a simple Git monorepo.
- Avoid Nx and Turborepo.

